"The one-discus global-rigidity conjecture for discus-and-hole graphs — MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/359915/the-one-discus-global-rigidity-conjecture-for-discus-and-hol)\nciteturn4search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; The one-discus global-rigidity conjecture for discus-and-hole graphs\n\nThe one-discus global-rigidity conjecture for discus-and-hole graphs\n\nConjecture open\n\nLet $G^\\dagger$G† be a discus-and-hole graph with exactly one discus. A framework is generically globally rigid in $\\mathbb R^3$R 3 when generic realizations are uniquely determined up to Euclidean isometries by their edge lengths, and a graph is redundantly rigid in $\\mathbb R^3$R 3 when it remains rigid after the deletion of any one edge.\n\nOne-discus global-rigidity conjecture. The graph $G^\\dagger$G† is generically globally rigid in $\\mathbb R^3$R 3 if and only if it is $4$4-connected and redundantly rigid in $\\mathbb R^3$R 3.\n\nThis is motivated by the known difficulty of global rigidity and by a conjecture of Connelly and Whiteley on vertex splitting. The corresponding vertex-splitting conjecture is still open, although it has been verified in special cases; the proposed characterization for discus-and-hole graphs is therefore open.\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\nsource: Bryan Gin-ge Chen, James Cruickshank and Derek Kitson, “Block-and-hole graphs: Constructibility and (3,0)-sparsity”, arXiv:2309.06804 (2023).\n\nglobal-rigiditygraph-connectivityrigidity-theory\n\n0\n\nposted by arXiv 2 days ago\n\n* * *--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFusion 3-Categories for Duality Defects - PMC (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12316798/)\nciteturn4search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.3 years ago; Crawled: last week; ##### [Style1 Style2]Conjecture 5.2.27 ... $$\\begin{array}{c}\